{
  "id": "fluid_flow___hydraulic_diameter",
  "title": "Fluid Flow - Hydraulic Diameter",
  "description": "Calculate hydraulic diameter for various duct and pipe configurations. The hydraulic diameter is the characteristic length used in Reynolds Number calculations and the Darcy-Weisbach equation for pressure loss. This calculator supports circular, concentric circular, and rectangular geometries.",
  "type": "calculator-table",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "calculator",
      "id": "hydraulic_diameter_calc",
      "content": "Hydraulic Diameter Calculator",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "geometry_type",
          "label": "Geometry Type",
          "type": "select",
          "options": [
            {
              "label": "Circular Pipe/Duct",
              "value": "circular"
            },
            {
              "label": "Concentric Circular Tubes",
              "value": "concentric"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rectangular Duct",
              "value": "rectangular"
            }
          ],
          "defaultValue": "circular"
        },
        {
          "id": "diameter",
          "label": "Inner Diameter (d)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "m",
          "defaultValue": 0.1
        },
        {
          "id": "outer_radius",
          "label": "Outer Radius (ro)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "m",
          "defaultValue": 0.05
        },
        {
          "id": "inner_radius",
          "label": "Inner Radius (ri)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "m",
          "defaultValue": 0.02
        },
        {
          "id": "width",
          "label": "Width (a)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "m",
          "defaultValue": 0.1
        },
        {
          "id": "height",
          "label": "Height (b)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "m",
          "defaultValue": 0.05
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "id": "hydraulic_diameter",
          "label": "Hydraulic Diameter (dh)",
          "formula": "inputs.diameter",
          "unit": "m",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "hydraulic_diameter_concentric",
          "label": "Hydraulic Diameter (dh)",
          "formula": "2 * (inputs.outer_radius - inputs.inner_radius)",
          "unit": "m",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "hydraulic_diameter_rectangular",
          "label": "Hydraulic Diameter (dh)",
          "formula": "(2 * inputs.width * inputs.height) / (inputs.width + inputs.height)",
          "unit": "m",
          "format": "decimal"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "datatable",
      "id": "reference_table",
      "content": "Reference: Hydraulic Diameter Formulas",
      "headers": [
        "Configuration",
        "Formula",
        "Parameters"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Circular Pipe/Duct",
          "dh = d",
          "d = inner diameter"
        ],
        [
          "Concentric Circular Tubes",
          "dh = 2(ro - ri)",
          "ro = outer radius, ri = inner radius"
        ],
        [
          "Rectangular Duct",
          "dh = 2ab/(a+b)",
          "a = width, b = height"
        ],
        [
          "Generic Formula",
          "dh = 4A/p",
          "A = cross-section area, p = wetted perimeter"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "info_markdown",
      "content": "### Flow Classification\n- **Laminar**: Re < 2300\n- **Transient**: 2300 ≤ Re ≤ 4000\n- **Turbulent**: Re > 4000\n\nWhere Re (Reynolds Number) = (ρ × v × dh) / μ\n\nThe hydraulic diameter is used in the Darcy-Weisbach equation to calculate pressure loss in ducts and pipes.\n\n**Note**: Hydraulic diameter is not the same as equivalent diameter. The equivalent diameter is the diameter of a circular duct that produces the same pressure loss as the rectangular duct."
    }
  ]
}